1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,038
Episode Sixty-One covered soft, weak, and phantom references.

2
00:00:04,038 --> 00:00:08,870
Reference types shape object lifetime — JVM flags shape runtime behavior.

3
00:00:08,870 --> 00:00:12,908
Every production JVM starts with dozens of implicit defaults.

4
00:00:12,908 --> 00:00:17,938
Heap size, collector choice, and logging flags change latency and stability.

5
00:00:17,938 --> 00:00:22,969
Copying flags from a blog post without measurement is a common failure mode.

6
00:00:22,969 --> 00:00:28,066
Today — JVM flags, heap sizing, GC switches, and a measurement-first mindset.

7
00:00:28,066 --> 00:00:29,911
Episode Sixty-Two.

8
00:00:29,911 --> 00:00:32,780
JVM Flags and Tuning Basics.

9
00:00:32,780 --> 00:00:35,630
Heap sizing starts with -Xms and -Xmx.

10
00:00:35,630 --> 00:00:40,430
-Xms sets initial heap — -Xmx sets maximum heap the JVM may use.

11
00:00:40,430 --> 00:00:45,005
Matching Xms to Xmx avoids resize pauses during steady state.

12
00:00:45,005 --> 00:00:49,206
Too small — frequent GC and OutOfMemoryError under load.

13
00:00:49,206 --> 00:00:53,556
Too large — long GC pauses and wasted RAM on shared hosts.

14
00:00:53,556 --> 00:00:59,406
Start from observed usage under realistic traffic — add headroom, not guesses.

15
00:00:59,406 --> 00:01:04,105
Collector flags select the garbage collector implementation.

16
00:01:04,105 --> 00:01:08,960
-XX:+UseG1GC — default general-purpose collector since Java 9.

17
00:01:08,960 --> 00:01:14,364
-XX:+UseZGC — low-pause collector for large heaps and strict latency.

18
00:01:14,364 --> 00:01:19,142
-XX:+UseParallelGC — throughput-oriented for batch workloads.

19
00:01:19,142 --> 00:01:24,624
MaxGCPauseMillis tunes G1 pause target — best effort, not a guarantee.

20
00:01:24,624 --> 00:01:30,184
Collector choice is empirical — validate with GC logs on your workload.

21
00:01:30,184 --> 00:01:33,843
Diagnostic flags make invisible behavior visible.

22
00:01:33,843 --> 00:01:38,324
-Xlog:gc* enables unified GC logging in modern JDK releases.

23
00:01:38,324 --> 00:01:42,655
-XX:+HeapDumpOnOutOfMemoryError writes a heap dump on OOM.

24
00:01:42,655 --> 00:01:46,539
-XX:ErrorFile=path captures fatal JVM error details.

25
00:01:46,539 --> 00:01:51,617
PrintFlagsFinal lists every flag and its effective value at startup.

26
00:01:51,617 --> 00:01:57,890
Flight Recorder and async profilers complement flags — use them before tuning blind.

27
00:01:57,890 --> 00:02:00,356
Tuning without measurement is guessing.

28
00:02:00,356 --> 00:02:04,845
Establish a baseline — latency, throughput, GC pause times, heap usage.

29
00:02:04,845 --> 00:02:08,765
Change one variable at a time — flag, heap size, or collector.

30
00:02:08,765 --> 00:02:13,128
Replay production traffic or run load tests that match real patterns.

31
00:02:13,128 --> 00:02:16,922
Compare before and after with the same dataset and duration.

32
00:02:16,922 --> 00:02:21,348
Document what you changed and why — future you will thank present you.

33
00:02:21,348 --> 00:02:25,173
A practical starter flag set for services.

34
00:02:25,173 --> 00:02:30,364
java -Xms4g -Xmx4g -XX:+UseG1GC -XX:MaxGCPauseMillis=200.

35
00:02:30,364 --> 00:02:36,284
Add -Xlog:gc*:file=gc.log:time,uptime,level,tags for GC analysis.

36
00:02:36,284 --> 00:02:43,478
Container deployments — respect cgroup memory limits with -XX:MaxRAMPercentage.

37
00:02:43,478 --> 00:02:49,033
Never set flags you cannot explain in an incident postmortem.

38
00:02:49,033 --> 00:02:54,862
Flags are tools — the goal is reliable behavior under real load.

39
00:02:54,862 --> 00:02:56,295
Three common mistakes.

40
00:02:56,295 --> 00:03:00,789
One — copying another team's flags without matching workload or heap.

41
00:03:00,789 --> 00:03:05,674
Two — setting Xmx to all available RAM — no room for metaspace or OS cache.

42
00:03:05,674 --> 00:03:10,364
Three — tuning GC before fixing allocation hotspots in application code.

43
00:03:10,364 --> 00:03:15,183
Also — changing five flags at once — impossible to attribute improvements.

44
00:03:15,183 --> 00:03:19,352
Measure first, tune second, verify third — always in that order.

45
00:03:19,352 --> 00:03:23,613
Interview question — how do you tune JVM flags for a service?

46
00:03:23,613 --> 00:03:28,573
Start with baseline metrics — latency percentiles, GC logs, heap usage.

47
00:03:28,573 --> 00:03:33,813
Size heap from observed peak plus headroom — match Xms and Xmx when stable.

48
00:03:33,813 --> 00:03:38,773
Choose collector for workload — G1 default, ZGC for strict pause goals.

49
00:03:38,773 --> 00:03:43,104
Enable GC logging and OOM heap dumps before changing anything.

50
00:03:43,104 --> 00:03:47,994
Change one knob, re-test, document — never tune from blog posts alone.

51
00:03:47,994 --> 00:03:53,536
Flags control the JVM — object layout controls how much memory each instance uses.

52
00:03:53,536 --> 00:03:57,321
Episode Sixty-Three — Object Layout and Compressed Oops.

53
00:03:57,321 --> 00:04:01,242
Headers, padding, and pointer compression on 64-bit heaps.

54
00:04:01,242 --> 00:04:02,188
See you there.
